WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:13.350
The thermite reaction which is the reaction
of aluminium and

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:21.109
iron oxide is used to make iron and because
aluminium has

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.060
much stronger bonds to oxygen than iron that
the reaction

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:30.029
produces an enormous amount of heat and you
can use this

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.890
for welding pieces of metals together.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.530
So this is a thermite mixture, it is a mixture
of iron oxide and

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.210
aluminium powder and we are going to do some
redox

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.219
chemistry and we are going to generate some
molten iron

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:46.030
which will hopefully stream from the bottom
of the flower

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.109
pot. So here you can see the black particles
of the iron oxide

00:00:50.109 --> 00:00:52.890
and the silver particles are the aluminium
powder. So we get

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.789
some heat on this and we start an instantaneous
redox

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:59.219
reaction so the aluminium and the iron oxide
swap oxygens

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:01.199
so the aluminium will become oxidised and
the iron will

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:04.460
become reduced hopefully we will generate
a lot of molten

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.420
iron which should come from the bottom of
the test tube …

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.101
of the flower pot.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.340
Alright!

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.460
Right.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.070
Now there is a story about students in Berkley
that some

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.810
students, or group of students, decided to
do a practical joke

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.219
on a tram of the sort that has one door where
you go in and

00:01:24.219 --> 00:01:29.009
another when you go out. So a big group of
students queued

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.180
onto the tram and got into the front and there
were enough

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.729
of them so they could actually come out at
the end at the

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.590
other door and form a loop so they were just
going round

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.179
and round and round so the tram could not
leave because

00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:43.920
they were just more and more people getting
on.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.229
We’re going to run out of tape.

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:56.880
And while they were doing this, other students
went down

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.249
under the tram and set off two thermite reactions
and welded

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:07.479
the metal wheels of the tram to the steel
rails so that when

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.830
the tram tried to leave, when the students
eventually stopped

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.040
going round and round, it was firmly welded
to the tracks and

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.160
could not move at all.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.630
It’s gone out.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:27.230
Tell us about what is going on mate!

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.489
So instantly the thermite reaction starts
and you can see that

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:34.269
it’s so hot that it burnt a whole through
the bottom of that

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:37.330
terracotta flower pot. But if we go in close
now Brady, you

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.599
can see all that really quite nice molten
iron. Now that is so

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:43.840
hot that the iron itself has melted and it
has formed this

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.780
really quite big goo in the bottom on the
sand.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.480
Do you encourage these sorts of practical
jokes?

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.680
No, no, no, no ,no, our students are far more
responsible.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.900
So as soon as the fire work, or the sparkler,
hit the top of the

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.610
thermite reaction, in the top of here, it
started obviously,

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.890
instantaneous redox reaction. So this is where
the metals

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.980
were fighting for the oxygen, the aluminium
won that battle

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.989
and the aluminium came out of this reaction
as aluminium

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.450
oxide. The iron was reduced and we can see
that now in the

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.769
bottom which is really hot so I am not going
to get too close

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:19.580
but you can see all of this really, really
hot iron. The iron was

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.201
molten, it dribbled out through the bottom
of the flower pot

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:23.360
and it is now cooling.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.591
It didn’t dribble out of the bottom of the
flower pot.

00:03:25.591 --> 00:03:28.810
Well it smashed its way out of the flower
pot.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.971
Well you can say that iron is in my blood,
it is in your blood

00:03:32.971 --> 00:03:36.920
as well. It is iron that gives haemoglobin
the red pigment in

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.040
your blood, its red colour.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.570
So the reaction we saw was about 5 minutes
ago and the iron

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:47.269
that was generated is still glowing red hot
and you can see it

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:51.079
has fused itself to the bottom half of the
flower pot, which it

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:55.840
broke in its tumultuous step forward.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.560
It is an absolutely essential element to life,
except for crabs

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.590
which use copper but you will have to hear
about that later.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.439
So iron is a really, really abundant metal
which is used in lots

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:10.700
and lots of structural material, so you get
to see lots of

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.650
pieces of iron around, some of the racking
and even in fact

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.900
some of these pieces of equipment have got
high iron

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.500
content. They have got other elements in them
to form alloys

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.930
like stainless steel.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:25.660
I have an extremely long standing interest
in its chemistry

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.890
and in particular, I made one compound of
iron so called iron

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:35.040
tetracarbonyl which had 4 groups round it
and everybody had

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:43.030
expected that it would have a shape like one
of these. Shaped

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.480
with tetrahedron like that whereas, in fact,
it had a much

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:52.220
more irregular shape, the 4 groups were arranged
like this,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:57.280
and so every since this, every time I hear
the word iron I get

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.170
quite excited.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:07.730
So this is iron wire, ok, and as you can it
is a very small wire.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:12.380
It is 0.2 in diameter and you can see the
shiny material

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.070
underneath is iron that has not undergone
oxidation so this is

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:19.170
where it has been protected from the oxygen
in the air now if

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.910
we look at the iron at the top we can see
that this looks really

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.600
familiar especially because it looks like
some of the rust which

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.540
we can see at the bottom of some of our cars.
So here is

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.490
some iron oxide on top of the iron.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:33.790
The very first chemical experiment I did the
chemical reaction

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.760
was with iron and I think the same is true
for many

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.520
generations of school children. I heated up
iron and sulphur

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.970
together and made iron sulphide sort of blackish
solid but

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.970
then we put acid on it and got a terrible
smell of bad eggs

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:54.850
and this was my introduction to chemistry
and I really loved

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:55.030
it.

